    "q-274555": {
      "dom_id": "step-q274555",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
      "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
