    "q-327994": {
      "dom_id": "step-q327994",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(4)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
